finding: Stage 4 overclaims causal attribution. Xid 109 naming Chrome identifies the faulting GPU channel, not whether engine work, Dawn compilation, browser interop or driver caused its timeout. Chromium 152 and Chrome 153 pin different Dawn (ab8827bc57b176eeaa89f71324130c02d4d41145 versus 28ffc61fc935c599677cff4dddfa4a12209ce500) and ANGLE revisions; clean 152 and Bun do not exclude a workload-dependent 153 failure. The headless probe explicitly forces ANGLE SwiftShader and Vulkan, fails vkCreateInstance with -7 (extension absent), and fails in both browsers; its shared shutdown message does not establish the headed NVIDIA hang/mapping failure's cause. Dawn 153 native Device.cpp:193-196 and wire/client/Device.cpp:206-209 both emit the message on callback shutdown, so the text alone does not prove GPU-process teardown or native provenance. Steam content/gameprocess logs and user journal show AION2 running 20:35:07-20:55:26 through all stage-3 Chromium runs and Chrome 20:54, with Fossilize graphics pipeline activity; instantaneous GPU load was not recorded. At 18:50/18:52 no Steam game is recorded (previous stop 01:06:20, next launch 20:35:07), so contention is not an established replacement cause either. Cause remains undecided; a controlled person-started headed A/B with game closed and recorded adapter/backend/submissions is needed. Swing retirement as unreproduced is acceptable, but headless mousemove-only tracing after device loss cannot prove the headed swings necessarily required Wayland post-lock motion or exclude headed synthetic input; core/input also consumes unlocked captured pointermove deltas, which the trace does not record.
